  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Thundercat Technology has $781M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Thundercat Technology has received 818 awards this fiscal year so far, with an average award size of $955K. Thundercat Technology's obligations are down 14.2% year over year. Thundercat Technology's recent fiscal history shows obligations of $566M in FY21, $680M in FY22, $854M in FY23, $804M in FY24, and $910M in FY25, before the current-year total of $781M. Thundercat Technology's top buying agency this year is the Department of Veterans Affairs at $301M, followed by the Department of Justice at $96.5M and the Department of Energy at $65.3M. Thundercat Technology's dominant market is Other Computer Related Services at $752M, with much smaller amounts in other NAICS categories such as Computer and Computer Peripheral Equipment and Software Merchant Wholesalers at $19.6M and Electronic Computer Manufacturing at $5.4M. This snapshot covers contract obligations only, except where award values are noted elsewhere on award tables.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Thundercat Technology's biggest federal customers?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is Thundercat Technology's largest buyer at $301M in FY2026 to date. The Department of Justice follows at $96.5M, then the Department of Energy at $65.3M and the Department of State at $64.7M. The Department of Commerce, Department of Defense, and Department of Homeland Security round out the top buyers with $57.1M, $47.9M, and $46M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What products and markets drive Thundercat Technology's revenue?",
        "body": "Other Computer Related Services is Thundercat Technology's largest market at $752M in FY2026 to date. Secondary markets include Computer and Computer Peripheral Equipment and Software Merchant Wholesalers at $19.6M and Electronic Computer Manufacturing at $5.4M. Smaller categories such as Software Publishers and Web Search Portals and All Other Information Services contribute $1.4M and $1.2M respectively."
      }
    ]
